{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause — criminal conviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly held liable for, assault or threats against the landlord, family or employees, theft on the premises, or human trafficking. A three-day notice to quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day notice; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the Eviction Law; grounds include conviction of assault or threats, civil liability for criminal activity, theft, and human trafficking.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"The civil-action ground does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside; suit must be brought within two years of delinquency, conviction, or release, whichever is later.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found by the court to be delinquent due to an offense involving assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, a member of the landl ord’s family or an employee of the landlord.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling evictions","requirement":"A tenant in a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by court order, and only for causes such as nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, damage or lease violations. Three months' notice applies to at-will and year-to-year tenancies, one month to month-to-month.","key_value":"3-month notice; 1-month for month-to-month","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal only by court order.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Any building with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms is a rooming or boarding house unless an exception applies.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","role":"covered","text":"rooming and boarding houses — buildings with at least two living units without private kitchens and bathrooms"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathroo oms is a rooming or boarding house"]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere; school and college dormitories; buildings housing only college students; certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction-notice misrepresentation","requirement":"A landlord taking an owner-occupancy eviction must occupy the unit at least six months; re-renting or converting makes the landlord liable for triple damages. A landlord who evicts to demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"3× damages + fees; civil penalty up to $10,000","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who evict tenants for personal occupancy or for demolition, boarding up, or permanent removal from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs  (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protections for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of the lease, or refusal to renew when the tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains in good faith to a government agency about health or safety violations, or joins a tenant organization.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction in New Jersey. Before evicting a tenant because a buyer wants to occupy the foreclosed home, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale that they need not move.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice of sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice; a lease running beyond 90 days bars eviction until it expires.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law providing longer time periods or additional tenant protections in foreclosure.","relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants at least 62 before conversion recording, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within the limit. The converter must notify eligible tenants.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income ≤ 3× county per-person average or $50,000","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership who meet age, disability, veteran and one-year residency requirements.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant must be at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or a veteran 60% disabled from service, and must have lived in the building at least one year before conversion; income must be no more than 3 times county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties — at present only Hudson County — in buildings converted or being converted to condominium or cooperative ownership.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","role":"covered","text":"qualified counties — at present only Hudson County"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b))."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","n_rules":8,"note":null}

